<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_BUNDLE_NAME}</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Simorg</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_EXECUTABLE_NAME}</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_GUI_IDENTIFIER}</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_ICON_FILE}</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_SHORT_VERSION_STRING}</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_BUNDLE_VERSION}</string>
    <key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
    <string>Copyright © 2026 Simorg. Licensed GPL-3.0-or-later.</string>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>12.0</string>
    <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
      A normal foreground app: Dock icon, and an application menu next to the
      Apple menu. Simorg still runs in the background with every window closed
      — that is what the status-bar item and setQuitOnLastWindowClosed(false)
      are for — but hiding it from the Dock as well left people with no
      familiar way to reach it or to quit.
    -->
    <key>LSUIElement</key>
    <false/>

    <!--
      Why Simorg is asking, in the words macOS puts in the prompt.

      These are not optional politeness. An agent is given folders by the person
      who owns them, and this application checks each one is there and readable
      before it hands the path to Docker — see AccessGrants::validate and
      AccessMounts. On a Mac that read is what trips the Files and Folders
      permission, so somebody granting an agent access to their Documents gets a
      system prompt naming Simorg. Without a string here the prompt explains
      nothing, and an unexplained request for the Documents folder is one a
      sensible person denies.

      Answering "Don't Allow" is recoverable: System Settings → Privacy &
      Security → Files and Folders. The app cannot re-ask.
    -->
    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Simorg asks before giving an agent a folder. This is for a folder in Documents that you chose to share with one.</string>
    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Simorg asks before giving an agent a folder. This is for a folder on your Desktop that you chose to share with one.</string>
    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Simorg asks before giving an agent a folder. This is for a folder in Downloads that you chose to share with one.</string>
    <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Simorg asks before giving an agent a folder. This is for a folder on a disk you connected that you chose to share with one.</string>
    <key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Simorg asks before giving an agent a folder. This is for a folder on a network share that you chose to share with one.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
